"""First-turn implementation and owner review surface, not a second simulator."""
from html import escape

def render_first_turn():
    beats = [
        ('01', 'Receive the mandate', 'Morning · producer console → leadership call', 'Answer the incoming call. Sixty robots by day seven. Dispatch earns funds; retained output grows the workforce.', 'Mandatory acknowledgement opens adviser selection.'),
        ('02', 'Trust someone', 'Roster → illustrated brief → assignments', 'Choose LIMEN or STILETTO. Read their proposal, accept it or swap the assignments.', 'The chosen voice sets the proposal. Their own room is delegated; elsewhere you retain the decision.'),
        ('03', 'Run the line', 'Shift · console → selected room → controls', 'Read one room through three CCTV angles. Take the controls: close/meter/open intake, hold steady/push the drive.', 'Cradles, work, clearance, waiting bodies, completed robots and equipment wear change together.'),
        ('04', 'Handle the pressure', 'Room evidence → response, when warranted', 'A full exit stops assembly. Load can create an incident. Back the adviser or override a response outside their room.', 'Time pauses for decisions. Actual outcomes and attributed reactions remain inspectable.'),
        ('05', 'Own the result', 'Dispatch → night → logistics', 'Keep produced workers, send them toward quota, or split them. Review the permanent order before sealing it.', 'The same worker IDs survive allocation. Payment, quota progress and wear reconcile.'),
    ]
    cards=''.join('<article class="principle"><span class="kicker">'+n+' / '+escape(where)+'</span><h3>'+escape(title)+'</h3><p>'+escape(action)+'</p><p class="small">'+escape(result)+'</p></article>' for n,title,where,action,result in beats)
    return '''<header class="section-heading"><span class="kicker">Prebuilt world → executable first turn</span><h2>Run it. Live with the result.</h2><p>One day connects adviser choice, physical production and permanent allocation in an inherited, prebuilt factory. The producer console anchors observation; focused workspaces carry the action.</p></header>
    <p><a href="/stepanoskin/loopforge/play">Play the current first turn ↗</a> · <a href="FIRST_TURN_BUILD_PLAN.md">Build checklist ↗</a> · <a href="FIRST_TURN_ENGINE.md">Engine contract ↗</a></p>
    <div class="principle-grid">'''+cards+'''</div>
    <p><a href="/stepanoskin/loopforge/design#agents">Next: agent art, motion and interactions →</a></p><h3>The room is the evidence.</h3><div class="scale-lenses"><article class="principle"><span class="kicker">FLOW</span><h4>Where is work waiting?</h4><p>Arrival and queue context. Change the input only when the rest of the chain can use it.</p></article><article class="principle"><span class="kicker">WORK</span><h4>What are bodies doing?</h4><p>Workers, machinery and the current assembly. See working, carrying, waiting and a real casualty where one occurs.</p></article><article class="principle"><span class="kicker">EXIT</span><h4>What actually leaves?</h4><p>The finite outtake and clearance threshold. A blocked exit has physical consequences, not just a warning badge.</p></article></div>
    <div class="callout">Three cameras, one room and one state. Angles are physical viewpoints. They are not the overview/work/close detail tiers, and they never make separate production decisions.</div>
    <h3>Concrete first choices</h3><table class="pairs"><tbody><tr><th>Opening facts</th><td>240 funds, 24 workers, condition 82. Security and the conveyor already exist. No construction expense; the later use of cash needs a separate economy pass.</td></tr><tr><th>Operating controls</th><td>Closed/metered/open intake and steady/pushed drive. Supervisor capacity, Security clearance and finite buffers constrain throughput. More input alone is not a win.</td></tr><tr><th>Dispatch</th><td>Each delivered basic robot adds one to quota and 12 funds. Each retained robot adds one worker and forgoes that payment.</td></tr><tr><th>Time</th><td>48 production beats, 50 ms fixed spatial steps; response-paced batches. Reading is paused. First play targets 2–3 minutes; repeat cadence still needs owner playtesting.</td></tr></tbody></table>
    <h3>What this slice must prove</h3><ul><li>You can explain the benefit and cost of your own opening choices.</li><li>A control change is visible in physical work and later totals.</li><li>Adviser placement changes capability and authority.</li><li>Switching rooms, angles, console or settings never resets the world.</li><li>Phone layout remains operable and the full camera composition meets its measured budget.</li></ul>
    <p class="small">Prototype values, not final balance. This ends at the first settled day. Later rooms, full BDI, wages, persistent runs and the multi-day progression remain future work. Unit checks do not certify visual quality or enjoyment; review the checklist and recorded evidence.</p>'''
